// In-depth material (part 4 of the Philippines): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
independence:{s:[
["The Spanish–American War","In April 1898 the United States went to war with Spain over Cuba. On 1 May Commodore George Dewey destroyed the Spanish fleet in Manila Bay. Dewey helped bring Aguinaldo back from Hong Kong, and Filipino forces quickly took control of much of Luzon, surrounding Manila. Many Filipinos believed the Americans had come as allies in their fight for freedom."],
["The mock battle","On 13 August 1898 Spanish and American commanders staged a mock battle for Manila, arranged so that Spain could surrender to the Americans rather than to the Filipinos. Filipino troops were kept out of the city. In December, under the Treaty of Paris, Spain sold the Philippines to the United States for 20 million dollars, without asking Filipinos."],
["The Philippine–American War","Fighting broke out on 4 February 1899 when an American sentry fired on Filipino soldiers in Manila. The war lasted officially until 1902, with guerrilla resistance continuing for years after. American forces used harsh methods, including burning villages and moving civilians into camps. The Balangiga incident in Samar in 1901 and the brutal reprisals that followed are among its most remembered events."],
["Steps toward self-rule","Americans promised to prepare Filipinos for self-government. The Philippine Assembly, elected in 1907, was led by Sergio Osmeña and Manuel Quezon. The Jones Law of 1916 promised independence once a stable government existed. The Tydings–McDuffie Act of 1934 set a ten-year transition under the Commonwealth, ending on 4 July 1946."],
["Independence at last","On 4 July 1946, at the Luneta in Manila, the American flag was lowered and the Philippine flag raised as Manuel Roxas became the first president of the Republic of the Philippines. The country was devastated by war. Agreements gave the United States military bases and trade privileges, which some Filipinos felt limited true independence."],
["Independence Day today","President Diosdado Macapagal moved Independence Day to 12 June in 1962 to honor the revolutionaries of 1898. Each year, the president leads a flag-raising ceremony, often at Kawit or the Rizal Monument, and towns hold parades, cultural shows and fireworks. Families display flags at home during the national flag days from 28 May to 12 June."]],
sp:["The Aguinaldo Shrine","Emilio Aguinaldo’s family home in Kawit, Cavite, where independence was proclaimed, became a national shrine in 1964. Aguinaldo himself redesigned the house, adding a tall tower and secret passages, and its ceiling is carved with a map of the Philippines. He lived there until his death in 1964 at the age of 94, having seen the country move from Spanish colony to independent republic. The famous balcony is a symbol of Philippine freedom."],
g:[["Independence","Freedom from control by another country"],["Treaty of Paris (1898)","The agreement by which Spain ceded the Philippines to the US"],["Commonwealth","The self-governing transition government of 1935 to 1946"],["Lupang Hinirang","The Philippine national anthem"]],
f:["Emilio Aguinaldo was 29 when he proclaimed independence and lived to be 94.","The Philippine flag is one of the few in the world flown upside down, red on top, in time of war.","The bells of Balangiga, taken by American soldiers in 1901, were returned to the Philippines in 2018."]},
phfest:{s:[
["Sinulog","Sinulog in Cebu City is one of the largest festivals in the country, drawing millions of visitors each January. It begins with a fluvial procession at sea, followed by a solemn procession of the Santo Niño and then the grand parade of dancers, drummers and giant floats. Its dance step imitates the current of a river: two steps forward and one back."],
["Dinagyang","Dinagyang in Iloilo City, held in January, honors the Santo Niño with tribes of dancers who paint their bodies and perform powerful, synchronized routines to thunderous drums. It began in 1968, when a replica of the Santo Niño was brought from Cebu. Dinagyang has won awards as one of the best tourism events in Asia."],
["Pahiyas and harvest festivals","Every 15 May, the town of Lucban in Quezon honors San Isidro Labrador, the patron of farmers. Houses are covered with colorful kiping, leaf-shaped rice wafers, along with fruits, vegetables and handicrafts. Similar harvest festivals take place in other towns of Quezon, and visitors are free to take some of the decorations after the procession."],
["Moriones","During Holy Week on the island of Marinduque, men dress as Roman soldiers wearing carved, painted wooden masks called morion. They roam the streets for days, playing tricks on children. The festival retells the story of Longinus, the Roman soldier who, according to tradition, was cured of blindness by Christ’s blood and converted."],
["Kaamulan and Indigenous festivals","Kaamulan in Malaybalay, Bukidnon, gathers the province’s seven Indigenous tribes, the Bukidnon, Higaonon, Talaandig, Manobo, Matigsalug, Tigwahanon and Umayamnon, for rituals, dances and traditional sports. Similar festivals, such as Lang-ay in Mountain Province and T’nalak in South Cotabato, celebrate local cultures and crafts."],
["The longest Christmas","The Philippines is famous for celebrating the world’s longest Christmas season, starting as early as September. Simbang Gabi, nine dawn masses from 16 to 24 December, ends with Noche Buena, a midnight feast on Christmas Eve. Children go caroling from house to house, and the season ends with the Feast of the Three Kings in early January."]],
sp:["The Giant Lantern Festival","In San Fernando, Pampanga, the Ligligan Parul, or Giant Lantern Festival, is held each December. Barangays compete with lanterns as tall as a three-story building, made of steel frames and thousands of colored lights. Inside each lantern, operators work rotors made of large steel barrels to switch lights on and off in dazzling, changing patterns set to music. San Fernando is called the Christmas Capital of the Philippines."],
g:[["Fiesta","A celebration, usually honoring a town’s patron saint"],["Parol","A star-shaped Christmas lantern"],["Simbang Gabi","Nine days of dawn masses before Christmas"],["Kiping","Colorful rice wafers used to decorate houses at Pahiyas"]],
f:["Nearly every one of the country’s more than 42,000 barangays holds its own fiesta.","Kalibo’s Ati-Atihan is often called the mother of all Philippine festivals.","Noche Buena, the Christmas Eve feast, often includes ham, queso de bola and fruit salad."]},
phfolk:{s:[
["Gods of the old religion","Before Christianity, many Filipino peoples worshipped a supreme being as well as lesser gods and ancestral spirits called anito or diwata. Among the Tagalogs, Bathala was the creator. Visayans honored Kaptan and Magwayen, gods of the sky and the sea. Offerings were made through babaylan, priestesses who led rituals and healing."],
["Biag ni Lam-ang","The Ilocano epic tells of Lam-ang, a hero who could speak at birth and chose his own name. He sets out to avenge his father, who was killed by enemies, defeats them single-handedly and then woos the beautiful Ines Kannoyan. When he is swallowed by a giant fish, his faithful rooster and dog help bring him back to life from his bones."],
["Hinilawod","The Hinilawod, from central Panay, is one of the longest epics in the world. Sung in the Sulod language over several days, it tells of the demigod brothers Labaw Donggon, Humadapnon and Dumalapdap and their adventures to win wives and defeat monsters. Much of it was recorded in the 1950s from elderly chanters by the anthropologist F. Landa Jocano."],
["Legends of places","Many places have legends explaining their names or shapes. The Chocolate Hills are said to be the tears of a heartbroken giant. Mount Makiling is the home of the beautiful goddess Maria Makiling. The legend of the pineapple tells of a lazy girl named Pinang whose mother wished she had a thousand eyes to find things, and she turned into a fruit covered with eyes."],
["The Darangen","The Darangen is the great epic of the Maranao people of Lake Lanao. It has 17 cycles and about 72,000 lines, telling of the kingdom of Bembaran and its heroes, such as Prince Bantugan. It is chanted at weddings and celebrations, often over several nights. UNESCO proclaimed it a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity in 2005."],
["Folklore and daily life","Folk beliefs still shape everyday customs. Many Filipinos will not sweep the floor at night, believing it sweeps away luck, or will avoid traveling soon after a funeral. Albularyo, folk healers, use herbs, prayers and massage. Pamahiin, superstitions, are often followed lightly as a mix of tradition and fun."]],
sp:["Maria Makiling","Mount Makiling, a dormant volcano in Laguna, is said to be guarded by Maria Makiling, a beautiful diwata, or forest spirit. In the stories, she helps poor farmers and hunters, gives them gifts of ginger that turn to gold, and punishes those who harm the forest. She withdrew from people after being betrayed. Rizal wrote down one version of her legend. Some say the mountain’s shape looks like a woman lying down."],
g:[["Anito","An ancestral spirit or the image representing it"],["Diwata","A nature spirit or deity"],["Babaylan","A priestess and healer in pre-colonial society"],["Epic","A long narrative poem about heroic deeds"]],
f:["Biag ni Lam-ang is considered the oldest recorded Philippine folk epic.","The Hinilawod has been recorded with about 29,000 verses.","In some folk tales the duwende can bring good or bad luck depending on how they are treated."]},
phecon:{s:[
["The economy since independence","After World War II, the Philippines was one of the more prosperous countries in Asia. But in later decades it fell behind neighbors such as Thailand and Malaysia, hurt by political instability, debt and corruption. Growth picked up strongly from the 2010s, averaging around 6% a year, and the country is aiming to become an upper-middle-income economy."],
["Manufacturing and exports","Electronics, especially semiconductors assembled and tested in economic zones, make up the largest share of Philippine exports. Other exports include machinery, wiring harnesses for cars, coconut oil, bananas, pineapples and copper. Special economic zones run by the Philippine Economic Zone Authority offer tax incentives to attract foreign companies."],
["Tourism","Tourism brings income to places such as Boracay, Palawan, Cebu, Bohol and Siargao, and supports many jobs in hotels, restaurants and transport. Before the pandemic, more than 8 million foreign tourists visited in 2019. The Department of Tourism promoted the slogan It’s More Fun in the Philippines, launched in 2012."],
["Agriculture and fishing","About a fifth to a quarter of Filipino workers are in farming and fishing, though these sectors contribute only around a tenth of the economy. Many farmers are poor, farming small plots. Rice, corn, coconuts, sugar, bananas and pineapples are major crops, and the country is a big exporter of bananas and pineapples. Land reform programs have redistributed millions of hectares."],
["Building the country","The government has invested heavily in infrastructure, such as new expressways, airports, bridges and railways, including the Metro Manila Subway and the North–South Commuter Railway. Better transport aims to reduce traffic, lower costs and spread growth beyond Metro Manila to regions such as Central Luzon, Cebu and Davao."],
["Poverty and opportunity","Poverty has fallen over recent decades, but millions of Filipinos remain poor, especially in rural areas and parts of Mindanao. Programs such as the Pantawid Pamilyang Pilipino Program, 4Ps, give cash grants to poor families who keep their children in school and take them for health checkups. Education, jobs and access to credit are seen as key to further progress."]],
sp:["The Philippine Stock Exchange tower","In 2018 the Philippine Stock Exchange moved to a new tower in Bonifacio Global City, Taguig, bringing together the trading floors once divided between Makati and Ortigas. Bonifacio Global City, built on a former military base, Fort Bonifacio, has grown since the 2000s into a modern business district with offices, shops, parks and the headquarters of many companies, a symbol of the country’s economic rise."],
g:[["GDP","Gross domestic product, the value of everything an economy produces"],["Remittance","Money sent home by workers abroad"],["BPO","Business process outsourcing"],["Economic zone","An area with special incentives for business"]],
f:["Overseas Filipino workers are often called bagong bayani, new heroes.","The Philippines is one of the world’s largest exporters of bananas.","It’s More Fun in the Philippines became a famous tourism slogan in 2012."]},
baguio:{s:[
["Burnham’s plan","In 1904 Daniel Burnham, the famous Chicago architect, drew up a plan for Baguio with parks, a government center and a market, carefully fitted to the hills. The plan included what is now Burnham Park and Session Road. The Mansion, built in 1908 as the summer residence of the American governor-general, is now the official summer residence of the Philippine president."],
["The 1990 earthquake","On 16 July 1990 a magnitude 7.8 earthquake struck central Luzon. In Baguio, hotels and buildings collapsed, including the Hyatt Terraces Plaza, and landslides cut off the city. Over 1,600 people died across Luzon, with hundreds in Baguio. Rescuers, including miners from nearby Benguet, dug through rubble for days. The city rebuilt within a few years."],
["The Panagbenga festival","Panagbenga, from a Kankanaey word meaning season of blooming, began in 1996 to lift spirits after the earthquake. Held through February, it features street dancing in costumes inspired by Cordillera culture and a grand parade of floats covered in thousands of fresh flowers. Session Road is closed to cars for Session in Bloom, filled with stalls and performers."],
["Pines and climate","Baguio’s cool weather comes from its altitude. Temperatures often fall to around 10°C in January and February, and the lowest recorded was 6.3°C in 1961. Benguet pine trees grow on its hills. But rapid growth has crowded the city and cut many trees, and traffic and water shortages are challenges for a city planned for far fewer people."],
["Markets and food","The Baguio City Public Market sells fresh vegetables grown in the highlands of Benguet, such as cabbage, carrots, potatoes and lettuce, along with strawberry jam, peanut brittle, ube jam and woven goods. Nearby La Trinidad is famous for strawberry picking. Baguio is also known for its cafés and the famous Good Shepherd ube jam, sold by nuns."],
["Mines Camp and the mountains","Baguio grew alongside the gold and copper mines of Benguet, one of the country’s major mining areas. Nearby attractions include Mines View Park, with views of old mining towns; the Bell Church, a Chinese-style temple; and Camp John Hay, once an American military base and now a resort and forest park. Hikers explore trails to Mount Santo Tomas and beyond."]],
sp:["The Philippine Military Academy","The Philippine Military Academy, at Fort del Pilar in Baguio, trains cadets to become officers of the army, navy and air force. Founded in 1936 and moved to Baguio, it is the country’s equivalent of West Point. Cadets study for four years under strict discipline. Graduation in March is a major event, often attended by the president. Its alumni include many generals and some national leaders."],
g:[["Hill station","A mountain town developed as a cool retreat"],["Ibaloi","An Indigenous people of Benguet and Baguio"],["Panagbenga","Kankanaey for season of blooming"],["Benguet pine","A pine tree native to the mountains of Luzon"]],
f:["The Mansion in Baguio is the official summer residence of the President of the Philippines.","Mateo Cariño’s land case reached the US Supreme Court, which ruled in his favor in 1909.","Baguio’s Burnham Park has a lake where visitors rent boats."]},
vigan:{s:[
["Calle Crisologo","Calle Crisologo, named after the writer and politician Mena Crisologo, is Vigan’s most famous street. Cobblestones laid in the Spanish era run between rows of tall ancestral houses with wooden balconies and shuttered windows. Cars are banned, and calesas, horse-drawn carriages, clatter along. Many houses now hold shops, cafés and small museums."],
["Churches and plazas","Vigan has two main plazas, Plaza Salcedo and Plaza Burgos. St. Paul’s Metropolitan Cathedral, built in an earthquake baroque style with thick walls and a separate bell tower, stands beside them. The bell tower was built apart from the church so that if it fell in an earthquake it would not damage the cathedral."],
["Burnay jars","Vigan’s burnay pottery tradition dates back to Chinese settlers. Potters shape large, dark earthen jars using clay from the Abra River and fire them in long wood-fired kilns called pugon. The jars are used to ferment and store basi, sugarcane wine, and bagoong, fish paste. Visitors can watch potters at work and try making pots themselves."],
["Abel Iloco","Ilocano weavers create abel, a strong cotton cloth woven on wooden looms in patterns such as binakul, which is said to confuse evil spirits. Abel is made into blankets, towels and clothing. The craft is passed from mother to daughter, and modern designers have revived it for fashion."],
["Elpidio Quirino","Elpidio Quirino, president of the Philippines from 1948 to 1953, was born in Vigan, in a jail where his father worked as a warden. His presidency focused on rebuilding after the war. A museum in Vigan, Syquia Mansion, the home of his wife’s family, displays his belongings and shows how wealthy Vigan families lived."],
["Vigan by night","In the evening, the Plaza Salcedo comes alive with a dancing fountain show set to music and lights. Visitors stroll Calle Crisologo under the glow of lamps, eat empanada and okoy at food stalls, and explore the Baluarte, a nature park on the city outskirts. The Viva Vigan Festival of the Arts in May celebrates the city’s heritage."]],
sp:["The Silang revolt","Diego Silang led a revolt in 1762 while British forces occupied Manila, hoping to end Spanish abuses in Ilocos. He was assassinated in 1763 by a former friend paid by the Spanish. His wife, Gabriela Silang, took command and fought on for months on horseback. She was captured and hanged in Vigan in September 1763. Gabriela is remembered as one of the first women to lead a revolt in the Philippines."],
g:[["Bahay na bato","A house with a stone lower floor and wooden upper floor"],["Calesa","A horse-drawn carriage"],["Burnay","Traditional earthenware jars of Vigan"],["Abel","Handwoven Ilocano cloth"]],
f:["Vigan is one of the few Hispanic towns in the Philippines whose layout survives largely intact.","President Elpidio Quirino was born in Vigan in 1890.","Vigan longganisa is small, garlicky and slightly sour."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-ph5.js */
// In-depth material (part 5 of the Philippines): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
boracay:{s:[
["Getting there","Most visitors fly to Caticlan or Kalibo on Panay, then take a short boat ride across the narrow channel to Boracay’s jetty port. From there, tricycles and electric vehicles carry them to their hotels. The island is small enough to cross on foot in less than an hour at its narrowest point, near the middle."],
["Beaches beyond White Beach","Boracay has more than a dozen beaches. Bulabog Beach, on the eastern side, is one of Asia’s best spots for kitesurfing and windsurfing during the northeast monsoon, called amihan. Puka Shell Beach in the north was once covered with tiny shells used for necklaces. Diniwid, Ilig-Iligan and Balinghai are quieter coves."],
["Under the sea","The waters around Boracay have coral gardens, reef fish, sea turtles and colorful nudibranchs. Popular dive sites include Crocodile Island, Yapak, where divers sometimes see sharks along a deep wall, and Friday’s Rock. Snorkeling trips, glass-bottomed boats and helmet diving let non-divers see the reefs as well."],
["The Ati of Boracay","The Ati are among the earliest inhabitants of Panay and nearby islands. As tourism grew, many Ati families were pushed off the best land. In 2018 the government awarded the Ati community land titles to a few hectares on the island, recognizing their ancestral claim. The Ati are also honored in the Ati-Atihan festival of nearby Kalibo."],
["Why it closed","By 2018 Boracay’s rapid growth had overwhelmed it. Untreated sewage flowed into the sea, causing algae blooms near the beach, and many buildings had been built illegally on wetlands and too close to the water. President Rodrigo Duterte called the island a cesspool and ordered it closed for six months, from April to October 2018, for rehabilitation."],
["The new Boracay","After reopening, Boracay enforced a 30-meter easement along White Beach, where no structures are allowed, and new sewer and drainage systems were built. The number of tourists allowed on the island at one time was capped, hotels must be accredited and visitors need confirmed bookings. Electric tricycles replaced many gasoline vehicles."]],
sp:["The paraw","A paraw is a traditional Visayan double-outrigger sailboat with a narrow hull and two bamboo floats on either side for balance. Its large sail, once woven from palm leaves, is now usually made of cloth. Fishermen sailed paraw for generations, and on Boracay they now carry tourists across the water at sunset. Iloilo holds the annual Paraw Regatta, one of the oldest sailing events in Asia."],
g:[["Amihan","The cool northeast monsoon"],["Habagat","The rainy southwest monsoon"],["Easement","A strip of land kept free of buildings, here along the shore"],["Paraw","A traditional Visayan outrigger sailboat"]],
f:["Boracay’s White Beach has been named the world’s best beach by several travel publications.","The island covers only about 10 km².","Boracay is part of the municipality of Malay in Aklan."]},
siargao:{s:[
["The towns of Siargao","Siargao has nine municipalities, including General Luna, the surfing and tourism center; Dapa, the main port; Del Carmen, with its vast mangroves; and Pilar, home to the Magpupungko rock pools. Many visitors fly into Sayak Airport in Del Carmen, while others arrive by ferry from Surigao City."],
["How the waves form","Siargao faces the open Philippine Sea, with deep water running right up to the reefs. Swells generated by distant storms in the Pacific travel thousands of kilometers before hitting the island’s reefs, where they rise suddenly and break. The best surfing season is from about August to November, when typhoon swells and offshore winds combine."],
["Mangroves of Del Carmen","Del Carmen has one of the largest continuous mangrove forests in Mindanao, covering more than 4,000 hectares. Mangroves protect the coast from storm surges, filter water and serve as nurseries for fish and crabs. Visitors paddle among the roots, and a crocodile sanctuary helps protect the saltwater crocodiles that live there."],
["Island life and food","Before tourism, most Siargao families lived by fishing and growing coconuts. Today many also run guesthouses, restaurants and surf schools. Local dishes include fresh kinilaw, raw fish cured in vinegar or coconut vinegar with ginger and onion, and grilled seafood. Coconut palms line the famous winding road toward Pacifico."],
["Surf culture","Siargao has produced its own surfing champions, many of whom grew up on the island’s waves. Surf schools teach beginners on gentler breaks, while experienced surfers ride Cloud 9, Stimpy’s, Jacking Horse and Pacifico. The annual international surfing competition draws professional surfers from around the world, and local surfers often take home prizes."],
["Growing pains","Siargao’s popularity has brought rising land prices, traffic, waste and pressure on fresh water. Residents and local governments work to manage growth, protect the protected landscape and seascape and keep the relaxed island character that draws visitors. Building rules and waste programs aim to keep the island from repeating the problems of other tourist hot spots."]],
sp:["Typhoon Odette","Super Typhoon Odette, known internationally as Rai, made its first landfall on Siargao on 16 December 2021 with winds of about 195 km/h. It flattened homes, tore roofs from resorts, snapped coconut trees and cut power and communications for weeks. The storm went on to devastate parts of Dinagat, Bohol, Cebu, Negros and Palawan, killing over 400 people. Within a year, much of Siargao had reopened to visitors."],
g:[["Reef break","A surf spot where waves break over a coral reef"],["Swell","Ocean waves created by distant winds"],["Mangrove","A salt-tolerant tree that grows in coastal waters"],["Kinilaw","Raw fish cured in vinegar or citrus"]],
f:["Siargao is about 437 km² in area.","Cloud 9 got its name from a chocolate bar.","Siargao was declared a protected landscape and seascape in 1996."]},
pinatubo:{s:[
["A forgotten volcano","Before 1991 most people did not even know Pinatubo was a volcano. Covered in forest, it was home to Aeta communities and lay near the American Clark Air Base. Geologists later found that it had erupted about 500 years earlier and even more violently around 35,000 years ago. Its awakening in 1991 surprised the world."],
["A successful forecast","Scientists installed seismometers and tracked thousands of small earthquakes and rising sulfur dioxide. They issued alert levels and produced hazard maps showing which areas were in danger. About 60,000 people were evacuated before the main eruption, and Clark Air Base was emptied. Experts estimate the warnings saved at least 5,000 lives."],
["Effects on the world","The eruption threw about 15 million metric tons of sulfur dioxide into the stratosphere. There it formed a haze of tiny droplets that circled the globe, reflecting sunlight. Sunsets around the world glowed red and purple for months, global temperatures dipped by about 0.5°C in 1992, and the ozone layer thinned temporarily."],
["The Aeta people","The Aeta, one of the Negrito peoples and among the earliest inhabitants of the Philippines, had lived on Pinatubo’s slopes for generations, hunting, gathering and farming. They believed the mountain was the home of their god Apo Namalyari. The eruption destroyed their villages, and many lived for years in resettlement camps before some returned to the mountain."],
["The Americans leave","Ash heavily damaged Clark Air Base, and the United States decided not to repair it. In September 1991 the Philippine Senate voted not to renew the treaty that allowed American bases, and Subic Naval Base, also covered in ash, was handed over in 1992. Clark and Subic were turned into special economic zones and an airport."],
["Lahar control","Over the following decade, lahars buried large areas of farmland and whole towns. Rivers such as the Pasig-Potrero and the O’Donnell filled with volcanic sediment. Engineers built a long dike, the Megadike, to protect towns in Pampanga. In 2001 the crater lake threatened to overflow, and a channel was cut to drain it safely."]],
sp:["The church of Bacolor","San Guillermo Parish Church in Bacolor, Pampanga, was built in the 1500s and rebuilt in the 1800s. In October 1995, lahars from Pinatubo swept through the town, burying the church up to half its height. Rather than abandon it, the people dug out the interior and raised the floor, so today visitors enter through what were once high windows. It is a striking reminder of the eruption’s lasting effects."],
g:[["Stratosphere","The layer of the atmosphere above the weather, about 10 to 50 km up"],["Lahar","A fast-flowing mudflow of volcanic debris and water"],["Caldera","A large crater formed when a volcano collapses"],["Aeta","An Indigenous people of central Luzon"]],
f:["The 1991 Pinatubo eruption was about ten times larger than the 1980 eruption of Mount St. Helens.","Pinatubo’s summit lost about 260 m in height in the eruption.","The Pinatubo crater lake is one of the deepest lakes in the Philippines."]},
lapulapu:{s:[
["Mactan before Magellan","Mactan was a small island of fishing and trading communities across a narrow channel from Cebu. It was divided among several chiefs. Lapulapu ruled one part, while another chief, Zula, was willing to submit to the Spaniards. Rivalries among datus and the powerful Rajah Humabon of Cebu shaped the events of April 1521."],
["Magellan’s demand","After converting Humabon, Magellan demanded that other chiefs accept the Spanish king and the Christian faith. He sent a message to Lapulapu demanding tribute. Lapulapu refused. Magellan, confident that Spanish weapons and armor would win easily, declined Humabon’s offer of warriors and insisted on attacking with his own men."],
["Weapons of the battle","Lapulapu’s warriors carried spears, bamboo lances hardened in fire, kampilan swords and shields. The Spaniards had muskets, crossbows, swords and steel armor. But firing from boats far out in shallow water, the Spanish shots had little effect. When the warriors learned that armor did not cover the legs, they aimed there, wounding Magellan in the leg with a poisoned arrow, according to Pigafetta."],
["After the battle","With Magellan dead, the expedition was shaken. A few days later, Rajah Humabon invited Spanish officers to a feast, where many were killed, possibly because Humabon’s attitude changed after the defeat. The survivors burned one ship and sailed on. Only one, the Victoria, under Juan Sebastián Elcano, returned to Spain in 1522."],
["Legends of Lapulapu","Because so little is known about Lapulapu, many legends grew. Some stories say he was an old man in 1521, others that he was young. Some Muslim communities in Mindanao and Sulu consider him a Muslim hero, while others question this. A popular legend says he turned into a stone that stands in the sea near Mactan. Historians treat these stories with caution."],
["Lapu-Lapu City today","Opon, the main town of Mactan, was renamed Lapu-Lapu City in 1961. Today it holds the Mactan–Cebu International Airport, beach resorts, an economic zone with factories and the Mactan Shrine, where the Lapulapu Monument and the Magellan Shrine stand near each other. The fish called lapu-lapu, a type of grouper, shares his name."]],
sp:["The Kadaugan sa Mactan","Each 27 April, Mactan celebrates Kadaugan sa Mactan, the Victory at Mactan, with a reenactment of the battle on the shore near the Mactan Shrine. Actors in traditional costume play Lapulapu’s warriors and Magellan’s men, wading through the water as the story unfolds. In 2021, for the 500th anniversary, the celebration was part of a national program marking the quincentennial of the battle and of Christianity in the Philippines."],
g:[["Datu","A chief or ruler of a community"],["Kampilan","A long, single-edged sword"],["Tribute","Payment made to show submission to a ruler"],["Quincentennial","A 500th anniversary"]],
f:["The grouper fish called lapu-lapu is a popular dish in Filipino restaurants.","Lapulapu is often called the first Filipino hero.","Lapulapu appears on the seal of the Philippine National Police."]},
bonifacio:{s:[
["Tondo beginnings","Bonifacio was born in a house near the Tutuban railway area of Tondo, then a crowded district of workers and fishermen. His father, Santiago, was a tailor and local official, and his mother, Catalina de Castro, worked in a cigarette factory. Their deaths from tuberculosis left Andrés, the eldest, responsible for five younger siblings."],
["Actor and reader","Bonifacio loved the theater and acted in plays, especially the moro-moro, dramas of battles between Christians and Muslims, which helped him become a confident speaker. He read widely, including the Bible, novels and books on law and revolution. He worked for British and German trading firms, learning about the outside world."],
["Founding the Katipunan","On 7 July 1892, the night after Rizal was arrested and deported, Bonifacio, Deodato Arellano, Ladislao Diwa, Teodoro Plata and others met in a house on Azcarraga Street in Tondo and founded the Kataastaasang, Kagalanggalangang Katipunan ng mga Anak ng Bayan, the Highest and Most Respected Society of the Children of the Nation."],
["Secrets and rituals","Members underwent a frightening initiation, were blindfolded and asked questions about Spanish rule, then signed an oath in their own blood. They used code names and passwords. Bonifacio’s code name was Maypagasa, meaning one with hope. Women joined in a women’s chapter, and Gregoria de Jesús kept the society’s seal and documents safe."],
["Kalayaan and the Decalogue","In 1896 the Katipunan printed one issue of its newspaper, Kalayaan, meaning freedom, on a secret press. It inspired thousands to join, and membership grew to perhaps 30,000 or more. Bonifacio wrote the Duties of the Sons of the People, and Emilio Jacinto wrote the Kartilya, which taught members to live with honor, kindness and courage."],
["The debate over his legacy","Some historians and citizens argue that Bonifacio deserves to be called the first president, since the Katipunan formed a revolutionary government with him as its head in 1896. His trial and execution by fellow revolutionaries remain a painful and debated episode. His remains were never found with certainty, but his memory lives in streets, schools and monuments throughout the country."]],
sp:["Gregoria de Jesús","Gregoria de Jesús, called Oriang, married Bonifacio in 1893 at age 18 and became the Lakambini, or muse, of the Katipunan. She guarded secret documents, carried messages and nursed the wounded. After Bonifacio’s death she searched for his body in the mountains. She later married Julio Nakpil, a composer and revolutionary, and wrote her memoirs. She died in 1943 and is honored as a heroine of the revolution."],
g:[["Katipunan","The secret revolutionary society founded in 1892"],["Supremo","Bonifacio’s title as head of the Katipunan"],["Cedula","A Spanish-era tax certificate and identity card"],["Lakambini","Muse; a title given to Gregoria de Jesús"]],
f:["Bonifacio Day, 30 November, is a national holiday.","The Katipunan’s flag went through several designs, including a red flag with a white K.","Bonifacio and Apolinario Mabini appear together on the Philippine ten-peso coin."]},
commonwealth:{s:[
["The 1935 Constitution","Delegates to a constitutional convention wrote the 1935 Constitution, approved by Filipino voters in a plebiscite. It created a presidential system modeled on that of the United States, with a president elected for six years, a one-house National Assembly, later changed to a two-house Congress, and a Supreme Court. It remained in force until 1973."],
["Manuel L. Quezon","Quezon was born in Baler, in what is now Aurora province, in 1878. He fought in the revolution, became a lawyer and rose to lead the Senate. He was famous for his passionate speeches and once said he would prefer a government run like hell by Filipinos to one run like heaven by Americans. Quezon City and Quezon province are named after him."],
["Social justice and land","The Commonwealth faced deep inequality, as many farmers worked as tenants for large landowners. Quezon’s social justice program set minimum wages, an eight-hour workday and courts to settle labor disputes. Peasant unrest in central Luzon, led by groups such as the Sakdalistas in 1935, showed the depth of rural anger."],
["A national language","In 1936 the National Language Institute was created to study Philippine languages and recommend one as the basis for a national language. It chose Tagalog in 1937, and from 1940 it was taught in schools. Quezon is called the Father of the National Language, and Buwan ng Wika in August ends on his birthday, 19 August."],
["Preparing for defense","Expecting war in Asia, Quezon asked General Douglas MacArthur to build a Philippine army. The plan called for training reservists, but there was too little time and money. When Japan attacked in 1941, Filipino and American troops fought together as the United States Army Forces in the Far East, but they were poorly equipped."],
["Refuge for Jewish families","Between 1937 and 1941, Quezon, working with the Frieder brothers and US High Commissioner Paul McNutt, helped about 1,300 Jewish refugees from Germany and Austria settle in the Philippines. Quezon donated land in Marikina for refugee housing. A monument in Israel, the Open Doors memorial, honors the Philippines for this rescue."]],
sp:["Quezon City","Quezon dreamed of a new capital away from crowded Manila. In 1939 Quezon City was founded on land in the hills to the northeast, planned with wide avenues, a central park and government buildings. It was officially the capital of the Philippines from 1948 to 1976. Today it is the country’s most populous city, home to the House of Representatives, the University of the Philippines Diliman and the Quezon Memorial Circle."],
g:[["Commonwealth","A self-governing transition government under US sovereignty"],["Plebiscite","A direct vote of the people on an important question"],["Tenant farmer","A farmer who works land owned by someone else"],["High Commissioner","The US representative in the Commonwealth"]],
f:["Manuel Quezon’s birthday, 19 August, is a holiday in Quezon City and nearby provinces.","The Commonwealth lasted just under eleven years.","Sergio Osmeña became president in 1944 when Quezon died in office."]}
});

;
