// In-depth material (History, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
silk:{s:[
["The secret of silk","Silk is made from the cocoons of silkworms, caterpillars that feed on mulberry leaves. The Chinese guarded the secret of silk-making for centuries, and smuggling silkworm eggs out of China was punishable by death. According to legend, monks smuggled eggs to the Byzantine Empire in hollow canes around 550 CE, starting a European silk industry."],
["Caravans and camels","Merchants traveled in caravans of dozens or hundreds of camels for safety. The two-humped Bactrian camel could carry heavy loads across cold deserts and mountains and survive on little water. Caravans moved about 30 km a day. They faced sandstorms, bandits, freezing passes in the Pamir Mountains and scorching heat in the Taklamakan Desert."],
["Oasis cities","Cities grew up around oases and river valleys along the route. Samarkand, Bukhara and Merv in Central Asia, Dunhuang and Kashgar in China, and Palmyra in Syria became rich trading hubs filled with markets, mosques, temples and inns. Merchants from many lands mixed there, and many people spoke several languages."],
["The Sogdians","The Sogdians, an Iranian people from around Samarkand, were among the greatest Silk Road traders between the 400s and 800s. They set up merchant communities across Central Asia and China, and their language served as a common tongue of trade. Some Sogdians rose to high positions at the Tang court in China."],
["Dunhuang’s caves","Near the oasis town of Dunhuang, Buddhist monks carved nearly 500 decorated caves, the Mogao Caves, between the 300s and 1300s. Their murals and statues show travelers, merchants and Buddhist stories. In 1900 a hidden library was found inside one cave, holding tens of thousands of manuscripts, including the Diamond Sutra of 868, the oldest dated printed book."],
["Diseases and exchanges","The Silk Road spread not only goods and ideas but also disease. The Black Death, a plague probably caused by bacteria carried by fleas on rodents, is thought to have spread from Central Asia along trade routes to Europe in the 1340s, killing a third or more of Europe’s population. Foods such as grapes, walnuts and carrots also traveled along it."]],
sp:["Marco Polo","Marco Polo, a merchant from Venice, traveled to China with his father and uncle in 1271 and stayed for about 17 years, serving the Mongol emperor Kublai Khan. Back in Europe, he was captured in a war and dictated his story in prison to a writer named Rustichello. The book described paper money, coal, huge cities and riches that amazed Europeans. Some scholars have doubted parts of his account, but it inspired explorers, including Columbus."],
g:[["Caravan","A group of traders traveling together"],["Caravanserai","A roadside inn for caravans"],["Oasis","A fertile spot in a desert with water"],["Sericulture","The raising of silkworms to make silk"]],
f:["China’s modern Belt and Road Initiative is named after the old Silk Road.","A single silk cocoon can be unwound into a thread nearly 1 km long.","The Silk Roads of the Chang’an–Tianshan corridor were listed as a World Heritage Site in 2014."]},
space:{s:[
["Sputnik shock","When Sputnik 1, a metal sphere with four antennas, began beeping from orbit in October 1957, Americans were shocked that the Soviets had taken the lead. The United States responded by creating NASA in 1958, increasing funding for science education and launching its own satellite, Explorer 1, which discovered the Van Allen radiation belts."],
["Animals in space","Before people, animals tested whether living creatures could survive spaceflight. The dog Laika orbited Earth in 1957 but died during the flight. In 1960 the Soviet dogs Belka and Strelka orbited and returned safely. The United States sent chimpanzees, including Ham in 1961. These flights proved that humans could survive weightlessness."],
["The Mercury and Gemini programs","America’s first astronauts, the Mercury Seven, became national heroes. Alan Shepard made the first American spaceflight in 1961, and John Glenn orbited Earth in 1962. The two-person Gemini missions of 1965 and 1966 practiced spacewalks, long flights and docking two spacecraft, skills needed for Apollo."],
["Tragedy and courage","Spaceflight was dangerous. In January 1967 three astronauts died in a fire during a test of Apollo 1. Months later, Soviet cosmonaut Vladimir Komarov died when his Soyuz 1 capsule’s parachute failed. In 1970 Apollo 13 was crippled by an explosion on the way to the Moon, but the crew returned safely after a tense four days."],
["The Soviet Moon program","The Soviet Union also tried to land cosmonauts on the Moon, but its giant N1 rocket exploded on all four test launches between 1969 and 1972. The Soviets instead sent robotic missions: Luna 16 brought back lunar soil in 1970, and Lunokhod 1, the first rover on another world, explored the Moon’s surface the same year."],
["Space stations and beyond","After the Moon race, both countries turned to space stations. The Soviets launched Salyut 1 in 1971 and later Mir, while the Americans launched Skylab in 1973. The Space Shuttle flew from 1981 to 2011. Today many countries, including China and India, have space programs, and private companies launch rockets and plan trips to the Moon and Mars."]],
sp:["Apollo 11","On 16 July 1969 a Saturn V rocket launched Neil Armstrong, Buzz Aldrin and Michael Collins toward the Moon. On 20 July the lunar module Eagle landed in the Sea of Tranquility with less than a minute of fuel to spare. Hours later Armstrong stepped onto the surface, followed by Aldrin, while Collins orbited above. About 600 million people watched on television. They planted a flag, collected rocks and left a plaque reading “We came in peace for all mankind.”"],
g:[["Cosmonaut","A Russian or Soviet astronaut"],["Lunar module","The spacecraft that landed on the Moon"],["Orbit","The curved path of an object around a planet or star"],["Satellite","An object that orbits a planet"]],
f:["The Apollo Guidance Computer had less memory than a modern pocket calculator.","Gagarin’s flight in 1961 lasted 108 minutes.","Footprints left on the Moon could last millions of years because there is no wind to erase them."]},
greece:{s:[
["Minoans and Mycenaeans","Before classical Greece, the Minoans built palaces on Crete, such as Knossos, around 2000 to 1450 BCE, decorated with colorful frescoes of dolphins and bull-leaping. On the mainland, the warlike Mycenaeans built fortress cities such as Mycenae. Their world collapsed around 1200 BCE, leading to a dark age before Greek civilization revived."],
["Democracy in Athens","In Athenian democracy, all male citizens could speak and vote in the assembly, which met about 40 times a year on a hill called the Pnyx. Many officials were chosen by lot. Citizens could vote to exile a dangerous politician for ten years by writing his name on pieces of pottery called ostraka, the origin of the word ostracism. Women, slaves and foreigners could not vote."],
["Life in Sparta","Spartan boys left home at seven to train in the agoge, a tough military school. They learned to endure hunger, cold and pain and became full-time soldiers. Spartan women were unusually free, exercising in public and running households. Most work was done by helots, an enslaved population who greatly outnumbered the Spartans."],
["The Golden Age of Athens","After defeating Persia, Athens became rich and powerful, leading the Delian League of allied cities. Under the statesman Pericles in the 400s BCE, the city built the Parthenon and other temples on the Acropolis. Playwrights, historians such as Herodotus and Thucydides, sculptors such as Phidias and philosophers made it a center of learning."],
["Alexander the Great","Alexander became king of Macedon at 20 in 336 BCE. In about ten years he conquered the Persian Empire, Egypt and lands as far as India, never losing a battle. He founded many cities named Alexandria. He died in Babylon at 32 in 323 BCE, and his generals divided his empire, spreading Greek language and culture across the Hellenistic world."],
["Greek legacy","Greek ideas shaped the Western world: democracy, philosophy, theater, history writing, geometry, medicine and the Olympic Games. Greek architecture inspired buildings from the US Capitol to the British Museum in London. Many English words in science and medicine, such as biology, telephone and democracy, come from Greek roots."]],
sp:["The Parthenon","The Parthenon, built between 447 and 432 BCE on the Acropolis of Athens, was a temple to Athena, the city’s protector. Designed by Ictinus and Callicrates, with sculpture overseen by Phidias, it held a 12-meter statue of the goddess made of gold and ivory. Its columns bulge slightly and lean inward, subtle refinements that make it look perfectly straight. In 1687 an explosion of stored gunpowder badly damaged it."],
g:[["Polis","A Greek city-state"],["Democracy","Government by the people"],["Acropolis","A high citadel of a Greek city"],["Hellenistic","The Greek-influenced period after Alexander"]],
f:["The marathon race recalls a legendary runner who carried news of victory from Marathon to Athens.","Greek doctors still take a version of the Hippocratic Oath.","The ancient Olympic Games were held every four years for more than a thousand years."]},
mongol:{s:[
["Mongol warfare","Each Mongol warrior had several horses, so he could switch to a fresh one and ride great distances. Armies used signal flags and drums to coordinate movements. They learned to besiege cities using catapults and engineers from conquered lands, and they used terror to make cities surrender, though they rewarded those who submitted."],
["The Yam postal system","To control their vast empire, the Mongols built the Yam, a network of relay stations a day’s ride apart, where messengers could get food, rest and fresh horses. Messages could travel hundreds of kilometers a day. Officials carried a paiza, a metal tablet that gave them the right to use the system."],
["Religion and tolerance","The Mongols followed traditional beliefs in Tengri, the eternal blue sky, and shamans, but they generally tolerated other religions. Christians, Muslims, Buddhists and Taoists lived and worked at the Mongol court. Over time, Mongol rulers in different regions converted, with the Ilkhans and the Golden Horde becoming Muslim and the Yuan emperors favoring Tibetan Buddhism."],
["Kublai Khan and the Yuan","Kublai Khan, Genghis’s grandson, completed the conquest of China in 1279, defeating the Song dynasty. He built a new capital, Khanbaliq, on the site of modern Beijing, and a summer palace at Xanadu. He tried twice to invade Japan and also attacked Vietnam and Java, with little success. The Yuan dynasty ruled China until 1368."],
["The Golden Horde","In the west, Batu Khan, another grandson of Genghis, conquered the Russian principalities and founded the Golden Horde. Russian princes paid tribute to the khans for about 240 years. Moscow gradually grew strong by collecting this tribute and in 1480 finally stopped paying, ending the so-called Tatar yoke."],
["Mongolia today","Modern Mongolia is an independent democratic country of about 3.5 million people, with Ulaanbaatar as its capital. Many Mongolians still herd animals and live in gers. Genghis Khan is honored as the father of the nation, and the Naadam festival each July features wrestling, horse racing and archery, the three manly sports."]],
sp:["Genghis Khan’s rise","Temüjin’s father was poisoned when Temüjin was about nine, and his family was abandoned by their clan. They survived by fishing and gathering roots. As a young man Temüjin was captured and enslaved but escaped. He built loyal friendships and alliances, defeated rival chiefs and united the Mongol, Tatar, Merkit and other tribes. At a great assembly in 1206 he was proclaimed Genghis Khan. His burial place remains unknown."],
g:[["Khan","A Mongol ruler or chief"],["Steppe","A vast grassland plain"],["Ger","A round felt tent, also called a yurt"],["Khanate","A state ruled by a khan"]],
f:["At about 24 million km², the Mongol Empire was roughly the size of North America.","Some studies suggest millions of people today may descend from Genghis Khan.","The Mongols used gunpowder weapons learned from the Chinese in some of their campaigns."]},
frrev:{s:[
["The Old Regime","Before 1789 France was an absolute monarchy. King Louis XVI ruled from the grand palace of Versailles, built by Louis XIV. The clergy and nobility, together only a small part of the population, owned much of the land and paid few taxes. Peasants paid taxes, church tithes and dues to their lords, and many lived in poverty."],
["The storming of the Bastille","On 14 July 1789, Parisians fearing that the king would use troops to crush the National Assembly stormed the Bastille, a fortress prison that symbolized royal tyranny, seeking gunpowder. They freed the few prisoners inside. The event became the symbol of the revolution, and 14 July is France’s national holiday, Bastille Day."],
["Rights of Man","In August 1789 the National Assembly adopted the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen, which said that men are born free and equal in rights, including liberty, property, security and resistance to oppression. Olympe de Gouges answered with a Declaration of the Rights of Woman in 1791, demanding equality for women."],
["The fall of the king","In 1791 the royal family tried to flee Paris in disguise but was recognized and stopped at Varennes. Trust in the king collapsed. In 1792 crowds stormed the Tuileries Palace, the monarchy was abolished and France became a republic. Louis XVI was tried for treason and guillotined in January 1793, and Marie Antoinette followed in October."],
["Napoleon","Napoleon Bonaparte, a young general from Corsica, won fame in Italy and Egypt and seized power in 1799. He crowned himself emperor in 1804. His Napoleonic Code organized French law and spread across Europe. His armies conquered much of Europe until defeat in Russia in 1812 and final defeat at Waterloo in 1815, after which he was exiled to Saint Helena."],
["Symbols of the revolution","The revolution created lasting symbols: the blue, white and red tricolor flag; the anthem La Marseillaise, composed in 1792; Marianne, a woman representing the republic; and the motto Liberty, Equality, Fraternity. The revolutionary calendar renamed months after nature and started counting years from 1792, though it was abandoned in 1806."]],
sp:["The guillotine","The guillotine, named after Dr. Joseph-Ignace Guillotin, who proposed a more humane, equal method of execution, was first used in April 1792. A heavy, angled blade dropped between two posts. During the Terror it became a grim symbol, used to execute the king and queen, nobles, revolutionaries who fell from favor and ordinary people accused of disloyalty. France continued to use the guillotine until 1977 and abolished the death penalty in 1981."],
g:[["Estates-General","An assembly of the three estates of France"],["Bastille","A Paris fortress prison stormed in 1789"],["Jacobins","A radical political club during the revolution"],["Republic","A state without a monarch"]],
f:["The metric system of meters and kilograms was introduced during the French Revolution.","La Marseillaise got its name from volunteers from Marseille who sang it as they marched to Paris.","Marie Antoinette probably never said “Let them eat cake.”"]},
wwi:{s:[
["The assassination","On 28 June 1914 Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne, and his wife Sophie visited Sarajevo in Bosnia. After a failed bomb attack, their driver took a wrong turn, and a young Bosnian Serb, Gavrilo Princip, shot them both. Within six weeks, Europe’s great powers were at war."],
["Life in the trenches","Soldiers lived in muddy, rat-infested trenches, often knee-deep in water, under constant threat of shells and snipers. Disease, lice and trench foot were common. Between the opposing lines lay no man’s land, full of barbed wire and craters. Attacks over the top often ended in slaughter by machine guns."],
["New weapons","World War I introduced deadly new technology. Poison gas, first used on a large scale at Ypres in 1915, caused choking and blindness. Tanks first appeared at the Somme in 1916. Airplanes were used for scouting and then for dogfights and bombing. Submarines sank ships, and zeppelins bombed cities."],
["The home front","Governments mobilized whole economies for war. Women took jobs in factories, farms and offices, replacing men at the front. Food was rationed, and propaganda posters urged people to enlist and buy war bonds. In many countries, women’s war work helped win them the right to vote after the war."],
["The Christmas truce","On Christmas Eve and Christmas Day 1914, along parts of the Western Front, British and German soldiers stopped fighting. They sang carols, climbed out of their trenches, exchanged gifts and cigarettes and, in some places, played football in no man’s land. Officers later banned such truces."],
["Aftermath and memory","The Treaty of Versailles of 1919 forced Germany to accept blame, give up territory and pay reparations, which caused resentment that helped lead to World War II. New countries, such as Poland, Czechoslovakia and Yugoslavia, appeared. The red poppy, from the poem In Flanders Fields, became a symbol of remembrance on 11 November."]],
sp:["The Battle of the Somme","On 1 July 1916 British and French forces attacked German lines along the Somme River after a week-long artillery bombardment meant to destroy the enemy defenses. It failed. On the first day alone, the British army suffered about 57,000 casualties, including nearly 20,000 dead, the worst day in its history. The battle dragged on until November, with over a million soldiers killed or wounded on both sides, for an advance of only about 10 km."],
g:[["Armistice","An agreement to stop fighting"],["Trench","A long ditch dug to shelter soldiers"],["No man’s land","The ground between opposing trenches"],["Reparations","Payments for war damage"]],
f:["About 65 million soldiers were mobilized during World War I.","The war was called the war to end all wars, but another world war followed 21 years later.","More than a million soldiers from India fought for Britain in World War I."]}
});

;
