// In-depth material (Technology, part 3): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
laser:{s:[
["The maser","Before the laser came the maser, which amplified microwaves instead of light. Charles Townes built the first maser in 1954. He and Arthur Schawlow then proposed extending the idea to visible light. Townes shared the 1964 Nobel Prize in Physics with two Soviet scientists, Nikolay Basov and Aleksandr Prokhorov, for this work."],
["The first laser","On 16 May 1960 Theodore Maiman at Hughes Research Laboratories in California fired the first working laser, using a synthetic ruby crystal and a powerful flash lamp. It produced brief pulses of deep red light. At first some people called the laser a solution looking for a problem, but uses were soon found everywhere."],
["Lasers in everyday life","Lasers read barcodes at supermarket checkouts, print documents, play and record discs and carry internet data through fiber-optic cables. Laser pointers are used in presentations. Lasers measure distances in construction and in cars that sense obstacles. Light shows at concerts use powerful colored lasers."],
["Lasers in medicine","Doctors use lasers to reshape the cornea to correct vision, remove cataracts, seal blood vessels, treat skin conditions and remove tattoos. Laser scalpels can cut tissue while sealing it at the same time, reducing bleeding. Dentists use lasers to treat gums and teeth."],
["Lasers in science","Scientists use lasers to cool atoms to temperatures near absolute zero, to build atomic clocks, to study chemical reactions that happen in quadrillionths of a second and to detect gravitational waves. Huge lasers at the National Ignition Facility in the United States are used for fusion research."],
["Lidar","Lidar sends out pulses of laser light and measures how long they take to bounce back, building detailed 3D maps. It is used by self-driving cars, surveyors, archaeologists who use it to find ruins hidden under forests, and scientists who measure forests, glaciers and coastlines. In 2018 lidar revealed more than 60,000 hidden Maya structures in the jungles of Guatemala."]],
sp:["Measuring the Moon","Apollo 11, 14 and 15 astronauts and two Soviet rovers left special reflectors on the Moon. Observatories on Earth fire laser pulses at them and time how long the light takes to return, about 2.5 seconds for the round trip. These measurements show the distance to the Moon to within a few millimeters and reveal that the Moon is moving away from Earth by about 3.8 cm a year. They have also tested Einstein’s theory of gravity."],
g:[["Coherent","Light waves in step with each other"],["Laser diode","A tiny semiconductor laser"],["Lidar","Measuring distance with laser pulses"],["Optical fiber","A thin glass strand that carries light"]],
f:["The word laser started as an acronym but is now an ordinary word.","Laser light can be focused into a spot smaller than a human hair.","The first laser was made with a ruby crystal."]},
chip:{s:[
["What a semiconductor is","Pure silicon conducts electricity poorly. Adding tiny amounts of other elements, a process called doping, changes this. Adding phosphorus gives extra electrons, making n-type silicon, while adding boron creates holes, making p-type silicon. Where the two meet, electricity can be controlled, which is how diodes and transistors work."],
["How chips are made","Chips are made on thin, round wafers of pure silicon. Layers of materials are added, patterned with light through masks, a process called photolithography, and etched away. This is repeated many times to build billions of transistors and wires. The most advanced machines use extreme ultraviolet light and cost hundreds of millions of dollars each."],
["Silicon Valley","The area south of San Francisco became known as Silicon Valley after companies such as Fairchild Semiconductor and Intel were founded there in the 1950s and 1960s. Many technology companies, including Apple, Google and Nvidia, are based there. The term now stands for the high-tech industry in general."],
["The chip industry today","Making the most advanced chips requires enormous investment. Taiwan’s TSMC is the world’s largest contract chipmaker. South Korea, the United States, Japan, the Netherlands and China are also major players. A shortage of chips during 2020 to 2022 slowed the production of cars, phones and game consoles worldwide."],
["Chips everywhere","Chips are found in phones, computers, cars, washing machines, medical devices, toys and credit cards. A modern car may contain over 1,000 chips. Special chips called graphics processing units, or GPUs, are used to train artificial intelligence. Tiny chips also power sensors in the Internet of Things."],
["The limits of shrinking","Transistors have shrunk so much that some features are only a few dozen atoms wide. At this scale, strange quantum effects cause problems, and making chips smaller is getting harder and more expensive. Engineers are stacking chips in 3D and developing new materials and designs to keep improving performance."]],
sp:["Moore’s law","In 1965 Gordon Moore, who later co-founded Intel, noticed that the number of transistors that could be placed on a chip was doubling about every year, a rate he later revised to every two years. This trend, called Moore’s law, held for about five decades and drove the rapid improvement of computers. The Intel 4004 of 1971 had about 2,300 transistors; today’s most advanced chips have tens of billions."],
g:[["Semiconductor","A material that partly conducts electricity"],["Transistor","A tiny electronic switch"],["Wafer","A thin slice of silicon on which chips are made"],["Photolithography","Printing circuit patterns with light"]],
f:["Silicon is the second most abundant element in Earth’s crust.","A single silicon wafer can hold hundreds of chips.","Taiwan makes most of the world’s most advanced chips."]},
radio:{s:[
["Maxwell and Hertz","In the 1860s James Clerk Maxwell, a Scottish physicist, wrote equations showing that electricity and magnetism are linked and that light is an electromagnetic wave. He predicted other, invisible waves. In 1887 Heinrich Hertz in Germany produced and detected radio waves with sparks, proving Maxwell right. The unit of frequency, hertz, is named after him."],
["Wireless telegraphy","Guglielmo Marconi, a young Italian, built on these ideas to send wireless signals in Morse code over longer and longer distances. He moved to Britain, set up a company and in 1901 received signals across the Atlantic. He shared the 1909 Nobel Prize in Physics with Karl Ferdinand Braun. Ships soon carried wireless operators."],
["The golden age of radio","In the 1920s to 1940s, radio was the center of family entertainment. People gathered around radio sets to hear news, music, comedy, sports, quiz shows and serial dramas. Leaders such as Franklin D. Roosevelt spoke directly to citizens in fireside chats. During World War II, radio carried news and propaganda."],
["Transistor radios","The invention of the transistor made small, cheap, battery-powered radios possible from 1954. People could carry radios to the beach or listen in their rooms, which helped spread rock and roll music among teenagers. Transistor radios also brought news and music to remote villages around the world."],
["Radio astronomy","Radio telescopes detect radio waves from space. In 1932 Karl Jansky discovered radio waves coming from the center of the Milky Way. Radio astronomers have discovered pulsars, quasars and the cosmic microwave background. Arrays of radio dishes, such as the Very Large Array in New Mexico, work together like one giant telescope."],
["Radio today","Radio is still popular, especially in cars and in rural areas. Digital radio offers clearer sound, and internet radio and podcasts let people listen anywhere. AM stations often broadcast news and talk, while FM stations play music."]],
sp:["The War of the Worlds","On 30 October 1938, Orson Welles and his Mercury Theatre performed H. G. Wells’s novel The War of the Worlds on American radio as a series of fake news bulletins about a Martian invasion of New Jersey. Some listeners who tuned in late believed it was real. Newspapers reported mass panic, though historians now think the panic was greatly exaggerated. The broadcast showed the power of radio to shape what people believe."],
g:[["Frequency","The number of wave cycles per second"],["AM","Amplitude modulation, varying a wave’s strength"],["FM","Frequency modulation, varying a wave’s frequency"],["Antenna","A device that sends or receives radio waves"]],
f:["Radio waves can be longer than a football field or shorter than a grain of rice.","The first radio broadcast of a sports event took place in 1921.","Radio waves from Earth have been traveling into space for about 100 years."]},
steam:{s:[
["Hero’s engine","About 2,000 years ago, Hero of Alexandria described the aeolipile, a hollow ball mounted on a boiler that spun as steam shot out of two bent pipes. It was a toy or curiosity rather than a useful machine, but it showed that steam could produce motion."],
["Pumping mines","By the 1600s English coal and tin mines were going deeper and flooding with water. In 1698 Thomas Savery patented a steam pump called the Miner’s Friend. Thomas Newcomen’s improved engine of 1712 used a piston and a large beam to pump water. Hundreds of Newcomen engines were built across Britain."],
["Boulton and Watt","James Watt, an instrument maker in Glasgow, partnered with the businessman Matthew Boulton in Birmingham. Their company made engines that could turn wheels as well as pump water, opening the door to factory machinery. Watt coined the term horsepower to compare his engines with the work of horses."],
["Steam locomotives","Richard Trevithick built the first working railway steam locomotive in 1804. George Stephenson’s Rocket of 1829 set the pattern for later designs. Steam locomotives grew more powerful over the next century. The British locomotive Mallard set a world speed record for steam of about 203 km/h in 1938, which still stands."],
["Steam at sea","Steamships replaced sailing ships during the 1800s, first with paddle wheels and later with screw propellers. Isambard Kingdom Brunel’s Great Eastern of 1858 was the largest ship of its time and laid the first lasting transatlantic telegraph cable. Steam turbines later powered fast ocean liners such as the Titanic and the Lusitania."],
["The end of steam","In the 1900s steam engines were gradually replaced by diesel and electric locomotives and by internal combustion engines in cars and ships. But steam is still everywhere: most electricity worldwide is made by steam turbines. Heritage steam railways around the world keep old locomotives running for visitors."]],
sp:["James Watt","James Watt was born in Greenock, Scotland, in 1736. While repairing a model Newcomen engine at the University of Glasgow in 1765, he realized that its cylinder wasted energy by being heated and cooled with each stroke. His solution, a separate condenser, made steam engines about four times more efficient. He also invented a governor to control speed and a copying machine. The SI unit of power, the watt, is named in his honor."],
g:[["Boiler","A container in which water is heated to make steam"],["Piston","A part that slides inside a cylinder"],["Condenser","A device that turns steam back into water"],["Horsepower","A unit of power coined by James Watt"]],
f:["Steam takes up about 1,700 times more space than the water it came from.","Mallard’s steam speed record of 1938 has never been broken.","Steam tractors were used on farms in the early 1900s."]},
scope:{s:[
["Galileo’s discoveries","Galileo built telescopes that magnified about 20 to 30 times. With them he discovered that the Moon has mountains and craters, that Venus shows phases like the Moon, that Jupiter has four large moons and that the Milky Way is made of countless stars. He published his findings in The Starry Messenger in 1610."],
["Bigger and better","Telescopes grew larger. William Herschel built large reflecting telescopes and discovered Uranus in 1781. In 1845 Lord Rosse built the Leviathan of Parsonstown, with a 1.8 m mirror, and saw the spiral shapes of galaxies. The Hooker telescope at Mount Wilson, with a 2.5 m mirror, let Edwin Hubble discover the expanding universe."],
["Mountaintop observatories","Earth’s atmosphere blurs and absorbs light, so observatories are built on high, dry mountains such as Mauna Kea in Hawaii, the Atacama Desert in Chile and La Palma in the Canary Islands. Adaptive optics, mirrors that change shape hundreds of times a second, correct for air turbulence and sharpen images."],
["Space telescopes","Telescopes in space avoid the atmosphere altogether. The Hubble Space Telescope, launched in 1990, has taken some of the most famous images of space. Chandra observes X-rays, Spitzer observed infrared light and Kepler and TESS searched for exoplanets. The James Webb Space Telescope, with a 6.5 m gold-coated mirror, sees farther back in time than ever before."],
["Radio telescopes","Radio telescopes use large dish antennas to collect radio waves. China’s FAST, with a dish 500 m across set in a natural hollow, is the largest single-dish radio telescope. Arrays such as ALMA in Chile combine many dishes. The Event Horizon Telescope linked radio telescopes across the globe to photograph black holes."],
["Amateur astronomy","Many people enjoy astronomy with small telescopes and binoculars. Amateurs have discovered comets, supernovae and asteroids. Clubs hold star parties to share views of the Moon, planets and nebulae. Light pollution from cities makes stargazing harder, so dark-sky parks protect places where the night sky can still be seen clearly."]],
sp:["The James Webb Space Telescope","The James Webb Space Telescope, launched on 25 December 2021, is the largest and most powerful space telescope ever built. Its primary mirror is made of 18 gold-coated hexagonal segments that unfolded in space. A sunshield the size of a tennis court keeps it extremely cold, so it can detect faint infrared light. It orbits about 1.5 million km from Earth and has photographed some of the earliest galaxies, star nurseries and the atmospheres of distant planets."],
g:[["Objective","The main lens or mirror of a telescope"],["Eyepiece","The lens you look through"],["Magnification","How much bigger an object appears"],["Observatory","A building or site for observing the sky"]],
f:["Hubble has made more than 1.5 million observations.","Isaac Newton’s first reflecting telescope was only about 15 cm long.","The Webb telescope can detect heat from objects as faint as a bumblebee at the distance of the Moon, NASA says."]},
robot:{s:[
["Robots in factories","Industrial robots weld, paint and assemble cars, electronics and appliances. They work quickly, precisely and without tiring, and they can handle dangerous jobs. Most are robotic arms fixed in place, guided by computer programs. Japan, South Korea, China, Germany and the United States use the most industrial robots."],
["Robots that explore","Robots go where humans cannot easily go. Rovers explore Mars, robotic submarines dive to the deepest ocean trenches, and robots inspect damaged nuclear plants such as Fukushima. Drones survey disaster zones, and robots defuse bombs. NASA’s Curiosity and Perseverance rovers have driven many kilometers across Mars."],
["Medical robots","Surgical robots, such as the da Vinci system, let surgeons perform delicate operations through tiny cuts using robotic arms controlled from a console. Robots also help in hospitals by delivering medicines, disinfecting rooms and helping patients with rehabilitation. Robotic prosthetic limbs can be controlled by signals from muscles."],
["Robots at home","Robot vacuum cleaners and lawn mowers do household chores. Smart speakers and toy robots respond to voices. Some robots are designed as companions for elderly people. Researchers are building humanoid robots that can walk, climb stairs and use tools, though they are still far less capable than humans."],
["Teaching robots to learn","Traditional robots follow exact instructions. Newer robots use artificial intelligence to recognize objects, navigate rooms and learn from practice or by watching humans. Self-driving cars are a kind of robot, using cameras, radar and lidar to sense the road. Making robots safe and reliable in the messy real world is a major challenge."],
["Robots and jobs","Robots and automation can make work safer and more productive, but they also replace some jobs, especially repetitive ones. New jobs are created in designing, programming and maintaining robots. Many experts say education and training are key to helping workers adapt as robots become more common."]],
sp:["R.U.R. and the word robot","In 1920 the Czech writer Karel Čapek wrote a play called R.U.R., short for Rossum’s Universal Robots. In it, a factory makes artificial workers to do people’s labor, but the robots eventually rebel. The word robot came from robota, a Czech word for forced labor, and was suggested by Čapek’s brother Josef. The play was performed around the world and gave the English language a new word."],
g:[["Robot","A machine that senses, decides and acts"],["Actuator","A motor or device that makes a robot move"],["Sensor","A device that detects light, sound, touch or other information"],["Automation","Using machines to do tasks without humans"]],
f:["The word robotics was first used by science fiction writer Isaac Asimov in 1941.","Some robots can move with insect-like legs or swim like fish.","More than 3 million industrial robots work in factories worldwide."]}
});

;
