// In-depth material (Technology, part 4): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
crypto:{s:[
["Scytales and substitution","Ancient Spartans wrapped a strip of leather around a rod called a scytale and wrote along it; unwrapped, the letters were jumbled until wound on a rod of the same thickness. Substitution ciphers replace each letter with another letter or symbol. Mary, Queen of Scots, used one to plot against Elizabeth I in 1586, but codebreakers cracked it and she was executed."],
["The Vigenère cipher","In the 1500s a cipher using a keyword to shift letters by different amounts was developed and later named after Blaise de Vigenère. Because each letter could be encrypted several ways, it resisted frequency analysis and was called le chiffre indéchiffrable, the unbreakable cipher. In the 1800s Charles Babbage and Friedrich Kasiski showed how to break it."],
["Navajo code talkers","In World War II, the United States Marines used Navajo speakers to send messages in a code based on the Navajo language, which was unwritten and almost unknown outside the Navajo Nation. The code talkers could encode, send and decode messages in seconds, and the Japanese never broke the code. Their work stayed secret until 1968."],
["How public keys work","In public-key cryptography, each person has two linked keys: a public key that anyone can use to lock a message and a private key, kept secret, that is the only way to unlock it. It is like a mailbox that anyone can drop letters into but only the owner can open. This lets strangers communicate securely over the internet."],
["Passwords and security","Websites store passwords as hashes, scrambled versions that cannot easily be turned back into the original. Strong passwords are long and hard to guess, and using a different one for each site limits damage if one is stolen. Two-factor authentication, such as a code sent to your phone, adds another layer of protection."],
["Cryptocurrencies","Cryptocurrencies such as Bitcoin, launched in 2009, use cryptography to record transactions in a shared public ledger called a blockchain, without a central bank. Digital signatures prove who owns coins. Cryptocurrencies are popular with some investors, but their prices swing wildly, and scams are common, so regulators urge caution."]],
sp:["Breaking Enigma","The German Enigma machine had rotors that changed the cipher with every key press, giving an astronomical number of possible settings, changed daily. In the early 1930s Polish mathematicians Marian Rejewski, Jerzy Różycki and Henryk Zygalski first broke Enigma using mathematics. In 1939 they shared their work with Britain. At Bletchley Park, thousands of people, many of them women, used machines called bombes, designed by Alan Turing and Gordon Welchman, to read German messages, a secret called Ultra."],
g:[["Cipher","A method of scrambling a message"],["Encryption","Turning readable data into code"],["Key","Information needed to encrypt or decrypt"],["Hash","A fixed-length fingerprint of data"]],
f:["The word cryptography means hidden writing in Greek.","Bletchley Park’s work remained secret for about 30 years after the war.","The Caesar cipher with a shift of 13, called ROT13, is still used online to hide spoilers."]},
camera:{s:[
["The daguerreotype","Louis Daguerre’s process, announced in 1839, produced a sharp, mirror-like image on a silver-coated copper plate. Each image was unique and could not be copied. Exposures took minutes at first, so sitters had to stay perfectly still, sometimes with hidden head braces. Daguerreotype studios soon opened around the world, making portraits affordable."],
["Negatives and prints","William Henry Fox Talbot in England invented the calotype in 1841, which produced a paper negative from which many positive prints could be made. This negative–positive idea became the basis of film photography for over 150 years. Later, glass plates and then flexible film made photography faster and easier."],
["You press the button","George Eastman’s Kodak camera of 1888 came loaded with film for 100 pictures. When it was full, owners sent the whole camera back to the factory, which developed the pictures and reloaded the camera. Eastman’s slogan was You press the button, we do the rest. His cheap Brownie camera of 1900 cost one dollar."],
["Instant and color photography","In 1948 Edwin Land introduced the Polaroid camera, which produced a finished print within a minute. Color film such as Kodachrome, introduced in 1935, made color slides popular. By the 1960s and 1970s, color prints became common for family photos."],
["The digital revolution","Digital cameras use a light-sensitive sensor made of millions of tiny pixels instead of film. Images are stored as files and can be viewed instantly, edited and shared. Camera phones appeared around 2000, and smartphones made them the most common cameras in the world. Kodak, slow to adapt, filed for bankruptcy in 2012."],
["Photography as art and record","Photographers have recorded wars, such as Mathew Brady’s images of the American Civil War, and great events, such as the Moon landing. Artists such as Ansel Adams photographed landscapes, and photojournalists show the world’s struggles and joys, from revolutions and famines to the civil rights movement."]],
sp:["The first photograph","Around 1826 or 1827, Nicéphore Niépce, a French inventor, placed a plate coated with bitumen, a kind of asphalt, inside a camera obscura at a window of his country house. After an exposure of perhaps several hours or even days, the bitumen hardened where light struck it, recording a blurry view of buildings and a courtyard. Called View from the Window at Le Gras, it is the oldest surviving photograph taken with a camera."],
g:[["Lens","Curved glass that focuses light"],["Aperture","The opening that lets light in"],["Shutter","The part that opens and closes to expose the image"],["Pixel","A tiny dot that makes up a digital image"]],
f:["The word photography means drawing with light in Greek.","Early portraits rarely show smiles partly because exposures were long.","More photos are now taken every year than were taken in the whole 1900s."]},
ev:{s:[
["Early electric cars","In the 1880s and 1890s inventors in Britain, France, Germany and the United States built electric cars. Around 1900, electric cars were popular in American cities because they were quiet, clean and easy to start, unlike gasoline cars that had to be cranked by hand. Some taxis in New York were electric."],
["Why gasoline won","Cheap oil discoveries, better roads for long trips, Henry Ford’s affordable Model T and the electric starter, which removed the need for hand-cranking, gave gasoline cars the advantage. By the 1930s electric cars had almost disappeared, except for vehicles such as milk floats and golf carts."],
["The electric comeback","Concern about air pollution, oil prices and climate change brought electric cars back. Hybrid cars such as the Toyota Prius, launched in 1997, combined engines and motors. Tesla’s Roadster of 2008 showed that lithium-ion batteries could give long range and speed. By the mid-2020s, electric cars made up a large share of new car sales in China and parts of Europe."],
["Charging","Electric vehicles can be charged at home from a wall socket or a home charger, at workplaces and at public charging stations. Fast chargers can add hundreds of kilometers of range in about half an hour. Building enough charging stations is important for EVs to spread, especially in countries where few people have their own garage."],
["Electric two- and three-wheelers","In Asia, electric scooters, motorcycles and tricycles are especially popular because they are cheap to run. India, Vietnam and Indonesia have growing numbers of electric two-wheelers. China has hundreds of millions of electric two-wheelers. They help cut air pollution and noise in crowded streets."],
["Electric buses and trains","Many cities are replacing diesel buses with electric ones. Shenzhen in China switched its entire bus fleet of more than 16,000 buses to electric. Electric trains and light rail are already common, and cities such as Santiago in Chile and Bogotá in Colombia now run large electric bus fleets."]],
sp:["Tesla Motors","Tesla was founded in 2003 by Martin Eberhard and Marc Tarpenning, and Elon Musk soon became its main investor and later chief executive. Its first car, the Roadster of 2008, used thousands of laptop-style lithium-ion cells. The Model S of 2012 and the cheaper Model 3 of 2017 made electric cars desirable and helped push the whole car industry toward electric vehicles. China’s BYD later became one of the world’s largest electric car makers."],
g:[["EV","Electric vehicle"],["Hybrid","A vehicle with both an engine and an electric motor"],["Range","How far a vehicle can travel on a full charge"],["Regenerative braking","Recovering energy when slowing down"]],
f:["An electric car named La Jamais Contente was the first car to pass 100 km/h, in 1899.","Electric motors can turn more than 85% of their energy into motion, compared with about 30% for gasoline engines.","Norway has the highest share of electric cars in new car sales of any country."]},
wind:{s:[
["Windmills around the world","Windmills spread from Persia to China, the Middle East and Europe. In the Netherlands, thousands of windmills drained land below sea level, creating polders for farms and towns. The windmills at Kinderdijk, built in the 1700s, are a World Heritage Site. In the American West, small windmills pumped water for farms and cattle."],
["Wind power for electricity","In 1887 James Blyth in Scotland and Charles Brush in the United States built early wind turbines to make electricity. Denmark became a pioneer, and in the 1970s oil crisis many countries began researching wind power. Modern three-bladed turbines, developed in Denmark, became the standard design."],
["Giant turbines","Modern wind turbines are among the largest machines ever built. The tallest stand more than 250 m high, taller than many skyscrapers, with blades longer than a football field. Bigger turbines catch stronger winds higher up and produce more power. A single large offshore turbine can supply electricity for thousands of homes."],
["Wind farms","Wind farms group many turbines together on land or at sea. The largest offshore wind farms, such as Hornsea in the North Sea, have hundreds of turbines. Engineers choose sites with strong, steady winds and connect them to the grid with underwater cables. Floating turbines can be anchored in deep water."],
["Wind leaders","Denmark built the first offshore wind farm, Vindeby, in 1991 and gets about half its electricity from wind. China has more wind power than any other country, and the United States, Germany, India and Spain follow. Britain’s Hornsea project in the North Sea is one of the largest offshore wind farms in the world."],
["Challenges","Wind power varies with the weather, so it must be combined with other sources and storage. Some people object to the look or noise of turbines, and they can harm birds and bats. Turbine blades are hard to recycle, though new designs are improving this. Despite challenges, wind is one of the cheapest sources of new electricity."]],
sp:["Vindeby","The world’s first offshore wind farm, Vindeby, was built in 1991 in shallow water off the Danish island of Lolland. Its 11 small turbines, each about 35 meters tall, produced just 5 megawatts in total, less than one of today’s giant turbines. It ran for 25 years and was taken down in 2017, having shown that wind farms could survive at sea. Offshore wind has since grown into a huge industry."],
g:[["Turbine","A machine with blades turned by wind or water"],["Nacelle","The housing at the top of a turbine"],["Offshore","At sea, away from the shore"],["Polder","Land reclaimed from the sea, kept dry by pumps"]],
f:["The power in the wind increases with the cube of wind speed.","Denmark gets about half of its electricity from wind.","Ancient sailors used wind power to cross seas thousands of years before windmills."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-sci1.js */
// In-depth material (Science, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
solar:{s:[
["The Sun","The Sun is a star, a giant ball of hot hydrogen and helium gas about 1.4 million km across, big enough to hold over a million Earths. In its core, at about 15 million °C, nuclear fusion turns hydrogen into helium, releasing the energy that lights and warms the planets. It is about halfway through its life of roughly 10 billion years."],
["Mercury and Venus","Mercury, the smallest planet and closest to the Sun, has almost no atmosphere, so its surface bakes at about 430°C by day and freezes at −180°C at night. Venus, similar in size to Earth, is wrapped in thick clouds of sulfuric acid and carbon dioxide that trap heat, making it the hottest planet at about 465°C, hot enough to melt lead."],
["Earth and Mars","Earth is the only planet known to have liquid water on its surface and life. Mars, the red planet, is a cold desert with the largest volcano in the Solar System, Olympus Mons, and a canyon, Valles Marineris, as long as the United States is wide. Evidence shows that Mars once had rivers and lakes, and robots search for signs of past life."],
["The giant planets","Jupiter, the largest planet, has a Great Red Spot storm bigger than Earth and at least 95 known moons. Saturn has spectacular rings of ice and rock. Uranus spins on its side, probably knocked over by an ancient collision, and Neptune has the fastest winds in the Solar System, over 2,000 km/h. All four have rings, though Saturn’s are by far the brightest."],
["Dwarf planets","In 2006 astronomers defined a planet as a body that orbits the Sun, is round and has cleared its orbit of other objects. Pluto, which shares its orbit with many Kuiper Belt objects, was reclassified as a dwarf planet. Other dwarf planets include Ceres in the asteroid belt and Eris, Haumea and Makemake beyond Neptune."],
["Scale of the Solar System","Distances in the Solar System are enormous. If the Sun were the size of a basketball, Earth would be a peppercorn about 26 m away, and Neptune would be about 780 m away. Light from the Sun takes about 8 minutes to reach Earth and about 4 hours to reach Neptune. Astronomers use the astronomical unit, the Earth–Sun distance of about 150 million km."]],
sp:["The Voyager probes","Voyager 1 and Voyager 2 were launched in 1977 to explore the outer planets. Using a rare planetary alignment, Voyager 2 visited Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune, the only spacecraft to visit the last two. Both carry a golden record with sounds and images of Earth, including greetings in 55 languages, in case they are ever found. Voyager 1 is now more than 24 billion km away, in interstellar space, still sending faint signals home."],
g:[["Planet","A large round body that orbits a star and has cleared its path"],["Dwarf planet","A round body orbiting the Sun that has not cleared its orbit"],["Astronomical unit","The average distance from Earth to the Sun"],["Asteroid belt","A region of rocky bodies between Mars and Jupiter"]],
f:["Saturn is so light for its size that it would float in a giant bathtub of water.","A year on Neptune lasts about 165 Earth years.","The Sun contains about 99.8% of all the mass in the Solar System."]},
photo:{s:[
["Discovering photosynthesis","In the 1640s Jan van Helmont grew a willow tree in a pot and found that the tree gained much weight while the soil lost very little, so plants must get their mass from somewhere else. In 1771 Joseph Priestley showed that a plant could restore air in which a candle had burned out, and Jan Ingenhousz showed in 1779 that plants need light to do this."],
["Chlorophyll","Chlorophyll is the green pigment that captures light energy. It absorbs mainly red and blue light and reflects green, which is why most plants look green. Plants also have other pigments, such as carotenoids, which are yellow and orange. In autumn, when chlorophyll breaks down in some trees, these other colors show, turning leaves gold and red."],
["Stomata and gas exchange","Tiny pores called stomata, mostly on the undersides of leaves, let carbon dioxide in and oxygen and water vapor out. Guard cells open and close them. On hot, dry days, plants close their stomata to save water, which also slows photosynthesis. A single leaf can have tens of thousands of stomata."],
["Food chains begin here","Almost all life on Earth depends on photosynthesis. Plants and algae are producers, making food from sunlight. Herbivores eat plants, and carnivores eat herbivores, so the energy in nearly every meal can be traced back to sunlight captured by photosynthesis. Even fossil fuels are the remains of ancient photosynthesizing life."],
["C4 and CAM plants","Some plants have special ways of photosynthesizing in hot or dry places. C4 plants, such as maize, sugarcane and many tropical grasses, concentrate carbon dioxide inside their leaves, which makes them very efficient in strong sunlight. CAM plants, such as pineapples and cacti, open their stomata only at night to save water, storing carbon dioxide to use during the day."],
["Artificial photosynthesis","Scientists are trying to copy photosynthesis to make fuels from sunlight, water and carbon dioxide. Devices called artificial leaves use catalysts to split water into hydrogen and oxygen. Researchers are also working to make crops photosynthesize more efficiently, which could help feed a growing population."]],
sp:["The Great Oxygenation Event","For the first two billion years of Earth’s history, the atmosphere had almost no oxygen. Around 2.4 billion years ago, tiny cyanobacteria in the oceans, which had begun photosynthesizing, released so much oxygen that it began to build up in the air. This Great Oxygenation Event changed the planet: it rusted iron in the oceans, wiped out many microbes for which oxygen was poisonous and eventually allowed complex, oxygen-breathing life, including animals, to evolve."],
g:[["Chlorophyll","The green pigment that captures light"],["Chloroplast","The part of a plant cell where photosynthesis happens"],["Stomata","Tiny pores in leaves"],["Glucose","A sugar made in photosynthesis"]],
f:["Phytoplankton in the oceans produce roughly half of the oxygen made on Earth each year.","Photosynthesis captures only about 1% of the sunlight that falls on most plants.","Some sea slugs steal chloroplasts from the algae they eat and use them to photosynthesize."]},
dna:{s:[
["Rosalind Franklin","Rosalind Franklin, a British chemist, used X-ray crystallography to take sharp images of DNA. Her famous Photo 51, taken in 1952, showed a pattern that revealed DNA’s helical shape. Without her knowledge, it was shown to James Watson, which helped him and Francis Crick build their model. Franklin died in 1958 and could not share the 1962 Nobel Prize."],
["Genes and chromosomes","A gene is a section of DNA that carries instructions for making a protein or another useful molecule. Humans have about 20,000 genes, packed into 46 chromosomes in most cells, arranged in 23 pairs, one set from each parent. Genes affect traits such as eye color and blood type, along with the risk of some diseases."],
["The Human Genome Project","Launched in 1990, the Human Genome Project brought together scientists from many countries to read all three billion letters of human DNA. It was declared complete in 2003, ahead of schedule. Today a whole human genome can be sequenced in a day for a few hundred dollars, compared with about three billion dollars for the first one."],
["Genetic engineering","Scientists can now cut and paste DNA to change living things. Bacteria have been engineered to make human insulin since 1982. Genetically modified crops, such as insect-resistant corn and golden rice, are grown in many countries. The gene-editing tool CRISPR, adapted from a bacterial defense system, has made precise editing faster and cheaper."],
["DNA and disease","Some diseases, such as cystic fibrosis and sickle-cell disease, are caused by changes in single genes. Genetic testing can diagnose them and help families plan. In 2023, the first CRISPR-based therapy was approved to treat sickle-cell disease. Doctors increasingly tailor treatments, such as cancer drugs, to a patient’s genes."],
["Ethics of DNA","DNA technology raises important questions. Should parents be allowed to edit the genes of future children? Who owns genetic information, and how should it be protected? In 2018 a Chinese scientist announced he had edited the genes of twin babies, provoking worldwide criticism and his imprisonment. Many countries have laws limiting such experiments."]],
sp:["Ancient DNA","DNA can survive for thousands of years in bones and teeth. Scientists have sequenced the genomes of Neanderthals and discovered the Denisovans, a previously unknown group of ancient humans, from a finger bone found in a Siberian cave. Svante Pääbo won the 2022 Nobel Prize for this work. Ancient DNA shows that many people in Southeast Asia and Oceania, including some Filipino Negrito groups such as the Ayta Magbukon, carry traces of Denisovan DNA."],
g:[["Gene","A section of DNA with instructions for a trait or molecule"],["Chromosome","A packaged strand of DNA"],["Genome","All the DNA in an organism"],["Mutation","A change in DNA"]],
f:["If all the DNA in one person’s body were stretched out end to end, it would reach to the Sun and back many times.","Identical twins have almost exactly the same DNA.","DNA from a 2-million-year-old ecosystem in Greenland is the oldest ever sequenced."]},
bh:{s:[
["Escape velocity","To escape a planet’s gravity, an object must travel faster than its escape velocity, 11.2 km/s for Earth. If an object is squeezed small enough, its escape velocity exceeds the speed of light, about 300,000 km/s, and nothing can escape. The boundary where this happens is the event horizon."],
["Stellar black holes","When a star at least about 20 times the Sun’s mass runs out of fuel, its core collapses in a fraction of a second while its outer layers explode as a supernova. If the remaining core is heavy enough, it collapses into a black hole. Our galaxy probably contains millions of stellar black holes."],
["Supermassive black holes","Nearly every large galaxy has a supermassive black hole at its center. The one in the galaxy M87 has about 6.5 billion times the Sun’s mass. Astronomers are still studying how such giants grew so large so early in the universe’s history, as the James Webb Space Telescope has found them in very young galaxies."],
["Quasars","When gas falls into a supermassive black hole, it swirls into a hot, glowing disk called an accretion disk and can shoot out jets of particles at nearly the speed of light. The brightest such objects, quasars, can outshine all the stars in their galaxy combined and be seen across billions of light-years."],
["Time near a black hole","Einstein’s general relativity predicts that time passes more slowly in strong gravity. Close to a black hole’s event horizon, time would slow dramatically compared with time far away. An observer watching something fall in would see it slow down and fade, never quite crossing the horizon."],
["Merging black holes","When two black holes orbit each other, they gradually spiral together and merge, sending out gravitational waves. LIGO first detected such waves in 2015 from black holes about 29 and 36 times the Sun’s mass merging 1.3 billion light-years away. Since then, scientists have detected dozens of mergers."]],
sp:["The first picture of a black hole","On 10 April 2019 the Event Horizon Telescope team released the first image of a black hole, the giant at the center of galaxy M87, 55 million light-years away. To capture it, they linked radio telescopes from Hawaii to Antarctica into one Earth-sized telescope. The image shows a bright ring of glowing gas around a dark shadow. In 2022 the team released an image of Sagittarius A*, the black hole at the center of our own Milky Way."],
g:[["Event horizon","The boundary around a black hole from which nothing escapes"],["Singularity","The point of infinite density thought to lie at a black hole’s center"],["Accretion disk","A disk of gas swirling into a black hole"],["Supernova","A powerful explosion of a dying star"]],
f:["The term black hole was popularized by physicist John Wheeler in 1967.","Black holes do not suck everything in; at a distance their gravity is like that of any object of the same mass.","The nearest known black hole to Earth is about 1,500 light-years away."]},
evo:{s:[
["Before Darwin","Long before Darwin, thinkers wondered whether species could change. In the early 1800s Jean-Baptiste Lamarck proposed that animals pass on traits they gained during life, such as giraffes stretching their necks. Geologists such as Charles Lyell showed that Earth was very old, giving time for slow change. Darwin’s grandfather Erasmus also wrote about evolution."],
["Natural selection explained","Darwin noticed that living things produce more young than can survive, that individuals vary and that some variations are passed on. Those with traits better suited to their environment are more likely to survive and reproduce, passing on those traits. Over many generations, this process, natural selection, can change a population and produce new species."],
["Alfred Russel Wallace","Alfred Russel Wallace, a British naturalist, independently came up with the idea of natural selection while collecting animals in the islands of Southeast Asia. In 1858 he sent Darwin an essay describing it. Their ideas were presented together that year, and Darwin hurried to publish his book. Wallace also identified the Wallace Line, a boundary between Asian and Australian animals."],
["Evidence from DNA","DNA provides powerful evidence for evolution. Closely related species have very similar DNA, and the pattern of similarities matches family trees built from fossils and anatomy. Humans and chimpanzees, for example, share about 98 to 99% of their DNA. Scientists can even estimate when species split apart from the number of differences."],
["Evolution in action","Evolution can be observed today. Bacteria evolve resistance to antibiotics, insects to pesticides and viruses change quickly, which is why flu vaccines are updated yearly. Peppered moths in England turned darker during the Industrial Revolution, when soot blackened tree trunks, and lighter again when air became cleaner."],
["Extinctions","Most species that have ever lived are now extinct. Earth has seen five mass extinctions, the most famous about 66 million years ago, when an asteroid impact helped wipe out the non-bird dinosaurs. Many scientists warn that human activity is now causing species to disappear far faster than normal."]],
sp:["Darwin’s finches","When Charles Darwin visited the Galápagos Islands in 1835 aboard HMS Beagle, he collected small birds that he later learned were different species of finch, each found on particular islands. Their beaks varied in shape: thick for cracking seeds, thin for catching insects. Darwin realized they had probably descended from one ancestor and changed to suit their food. Scientists Peter and Rosemary Grant later watched their beaks evolve within a few years during droughts."],
g:[["Evolution","The change in living things over generations"],["Natural selection","The process by which better-adapted individuals survive and reproduce"],["Adaptation","A trait that helps an organism survive"],["Species","A group of organisms that can breed together"]],
f:["Birds are living dinosaurs, descended from small feathered theropods.","Darwin waited about 20 years before publishing his theory.","The Wallace Line, between Bali and Lombok, roughly separates the animals of Asia from those of Australia."]},
atom:{s:[
["Early ideas","Around 400 BCE the Greek thinkers Leucippus and Democritus proposed that matter is made of tiny, uncuttable particles, which they called atomos. In 1808 John Dalton revived the idea with evidence from chemistry, suggesting that each element has its own kind of atom with a particular weight."],
["The electron and the nucleus","In 1897 J. J. Thomson discovered the electron, showing that atoms contain smaller particles. In 1911 Ernest Rutherford fired particles at thin gold foil and found that a few bounced back, showing that most of an atom’s mass is packed into a tiny, dense nucleus. If an atom were the size of a stadium, its nucleus would be the size of a pea."],
["Electron shells","Niels Bohr proposed in 1913 that electrons orbit the nucleus in fixed energy levels, or shells. The electrons in the outer shell determine how an atom reacts with others. Atoms with full outer shells, such as helium and neon, rarely react, which is why these noble gases are called inert."],
["Groups and periods","In the periodic table, rows are called periods and columns are called groups. Elements in the same group have similar properties. Group 1, the alkali metals, such as sodium and potassium, react violently with water. Group 17, the halogens, such as chlorine, are reactive nonmetals. Group 18 holds the noble gases."],
["Isotopes and radioactivity","Isotopes of an element have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons. Carbon-14, a radioactive isotope, decays at a known rate, so scientists can use it to date ancient wood, bones and cloth up to about 50,000 years old. Radioactive isotopes are also used in medicine to diagnose and treat diseases."],
["Mendeleev’s table","Dmitri Mendeleev, a Russian chemist, wrote the names and properties of the elements on cards and arranged them in order of atomic weight, grouping similar ones together. He left gaps for undiscovered elements and predicted their properties. When gallium, scandium and germanium were discovered, they matched his predictions, proving the table’s power."]],
sp:["Marie Curie and new elements","Working in a cold, leaky shed in Paris, Marie and Pierre Curie processed metric tons of pitchblende ore to isolate tiny amounts of two new radioactive elements, polonium, named after Marie’s homeland Poland, and radium, which glowed in the dark. Their work showed that atoms could change and release energy, overturning the idea that atoms were unchangeable. Element 96, curium, is named in their honor."],
g:[["Atom","The smallest unit of an element"],["Element","A substance made of only one kind of atom"],["Proton","A positively charged particle in the nucleus"],["Isotope","A form of an element with a different number of neutrons"]],
f:["About 99.9999% of an atom is empty space.","Hydrogen is the most common element in the universe.","There are more atoms in a glass of water than glasses of water in all the oceans."]}
});

;
